  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The offense went silent and never found a way back. Three hits and nine strikeouts against Houston is a shutout by another name, and the Athletics have now lost five straight. They trailed from the fourth inning onward and never mounted anything close to a threat late. That's the margin they're working with in the division. They can't afford nights like this, and they're getting more of them than they should.",
    "what_this_means": "Ginn threw five solid innings with three runs allowed, the kind of line that wins games when the lineup shows up. It didn't. The bullpen held clean for the final four. Alvarado struck out one in order, Rom and Juenger bridged to the end. But holding the opponent to three runs means nothing when you score zero. They left runners on in four separate innings, including two in the critical seventh with a chance to make it a game. That's not inconsistency anymore. That's a locked door. They've lost eight games of this kind since August began, and the division gap just grew to sixteen. The pattern isn't shifting. It's hardening.",
    "what_to_watch": "Hunter Brown takes the mound for Houston tonight with a 3.67 ERA, and Jacob Lopez counters after posting a 2.73 mark over his last four starts. Lopez has been the kind of stable arm the rotation needs. If he holds that line, the offense has to show something.",
